## Identity Reconciliation

Jira key is primary identity. Task artifacts and PR/MR metadata are searched for Jira keys and attached to matching identities. Records are not counted twice merely because they appear in multiple systems. Dashboard task list is complete for persisted Rehor tracking at collection time; it does not contain work performed locally before task tracking was deployed.

| Reconciliation result | Count |
|---|---:|
| Observed unique work identities | **{{ canonical_count }}** |
| Observed Jira-key identities | **{{ canonical_jira_count }}** |
| Observed non-Jira identities | **{{ unresolved_count }}** |
| Task Jira keys absent from baseline filter | **{{ missing_filter_count }}** |
| Baseline-filter Jira keys absent from task records | **{{ missing_task_count }}** |

### Interpretation

- Jira-key identities can exceed baseline filter because tasks and PRs reference Jira projects or keys outside filter `107017`.
- Filter issues can lack task records because work happened locally before deployment, or because issue/filter scope differs from persisted Rehor task scope.
- Non-Jira identities are Rehor work when present in persisted task records or authenticated bot Git activity. Their workflow type can be classified from task metadata, artifacts, config repositories, or Git history.

